WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.720
When it comes to investing, nobody&nbsp;
holds a candle to Jim Simons:

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.000
Not Warren Buffett

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.440
Not Steven Cohen

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.080
Not George Soros

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.600
Simons’ company Renaissance Technologies has&nbsp;
a signature hedge fund called Medallion that

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.040
has generated average annual&nbsp;
returns of 66% for 30 years.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.920
To put this another way, renowned economist&nbsp;
Bradford Cornell points out that if you had

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:29.960
invested $100 in Medallion in 1988, 30 years&nbsp;
later, that would have turned into $398.7 million!

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.480
What’s even more remarkable is that Simons hired&nbsp;
people who didn’t know a thing about investing!

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.720
Is it true that you never hired&nbsp;
people from the investment industry?

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.600
Never (laughter)

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.040
He hired mathematicians and&nbsp;
scientists, just like himself.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.240
While traditional investors rely&nbsp;
on their intuition to make trades:

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.480
chatting with executives, analyzing earnings&nbsp;
reports, gleaning insights from articles,

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.880
and considering geopolitical events,&nbsp;
Simons walked a different path.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:02.000
As a trained mathematician, he&nbsp;
believed markets moved in logical ways.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.600
So, he took a scientific approach to investing.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.600
Harnessing the power of vast&nbsp;
data sets, computer models,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:13.000
and algorithms to make decisions, inspiring&nbsp;
a revolution that has swept Wall Street.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.120
This is the story of how a mathematician&nbsp;
became the greatest trader who ever lived.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.480
Jim Simons is always restless.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.040
Building a storied career as a&nbsp;
brilliant mathematician wasn’t enough.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.400
Neither was teaching at MIT and Harvard

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.920
Neither was working as a Cold War code-breaker.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:32.960
He left Harvard to join an elite research&nbsp;
organization that helped America’s most

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:38.160
secretive intelligence agency, the NSA,&nbsp;
decode Soviet messages during the Cold War.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.040
But the work didn’t satisfy him.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:44.720
As his first wife Barbara recalled in Gregory&nbsp;
Zuckerman’s book The Man Who Solved the Market,

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.920
“Jim understood at an early&nbsp;
age that money is power.”

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.600
Growing up an only child in&nbsp;
Brookline, Massachusetts,

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.800
near Boston, his family doctor encouraged&nbsp;
him to consider a career in medicine,

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.160
remarking, "You're a bright Jewish&nbsp;
boy, you should be a doctor.”

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.920
But when Simons shared his passion&nbsp;
for mathematics, the doctor replied,

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.360
"Look, you can't make any money doing that stuff."

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.520
Simons always wanted to be rich.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.480
When he was fired from his code-breaking&nbsp;
job for protesting the Vietnam War,

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.560
he wanted to join an investment bank.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.040
He had three young children to support,&nbsp;
including a son who was born with a rare

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.280
hereditary condition that affected the development&nbsp;
of his skin, hair, sweat glands, and teeth.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.240
But, he opted to stay in&nbsp;
academia for the time being.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.880
He was offered a job heading&nbsp;
the math department at Stony

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.080
Brook University on Long Island at the age of 30.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.760
While it wasn’t as renowned then, here he was at

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:37.040
the age of 30 with the opportunity to&nbsp;
shape his own mathematics department.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.520
While at Stony Brook, he developed a&nbsp;
mathematical theory that is foundational

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:45.280
to the work of Microsoft and other companies&nbsp;
to create special quantum computers designed

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.280
to tackle challenges beyond the reach&nbsp;
of current systems, including in AI.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.120
As his professional life took&nbsp;
off, his personal life suffered.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.080
He and Barbara were young when they&nbsp;
got married, he was 21, she was 18.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:04.640
They divorced, and she headed west to complete&nbsp;
her PhD in computer science at UC Berkeley.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.240
A few years later, a lingering urge&nbsp;
nudged Simons to leave academia and

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:13.520
venture into the world of finance&nbsp;
by starting his own investment firm.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.760
His father thought he was making a huge&nbsp;
mistake by leaving a tenured position.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.360
Mathematicians were shocked and&nbsp;
felt he was wasting his talent.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.840
But Simons was always unconventional&nbsp;
- evidenced by small gestures like

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.040
not wearing socks because they&nbsp;
took too much time to put on.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:31.280
At the age of 40, he left his&nbsp;
position at Stony Brook to open

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.080
up a small office in a strip mall across&nbsp;
from a train station near the university.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:39.800
And so began Renaissance Technologies in 1978.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.480
Back then, it was called Monemetrics,

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:47.480
a play on money + econometrics, the use of&nbsp;
statistics and math to study economic data.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:53.040
Just as he once unscrambled enemy code, he&nbsp;
aimed to decode hidden patterns in the market.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:58.040
Simons remarked to Zuckerman in his book, “There’s&nbsp;
a pattern here; there has to be a pattern.”

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.360
He didn’t care why the patterns&nbsp;
existed, just that they did.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.160
Renaissance began by trading&nbsp;
currencies, commodities,

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.320
and bonds but avoided stocks due to&nbsp;
their complexity; there are so many

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.120
of them and each stock is influenced by&nbsp;
the unique circumstances of its company.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.240
Simons envisioned a trading system fully&nbsp;
guided by algorithms with no human input.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.640
Let's say you want to know what the weather will

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:24.400
be like tomorrow. You feed a computer&nbsp;
the information you have temperatures,

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.320
and humidity levels. The computer model&nbsp;
processes the information to predict

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.160
tomorrow’s conditions. An algorithm then&nbsp;
advises you on whether to carry an umbrella.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:37.240
Translated to the realm of quantitative&nbsp;
finance, the weather data becomes financial

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:41.400
data such as stock prices and trading&nbsp;
volumes. The weather forecast becomes

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:46.360
a market forecast. And the advice on what&nbsp;
to wear translates to a trading decision.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:50.880
On the other hand, a traditional investor might&nbsp;
try to predict the weather or the markets based

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.920
on subtle cues in their environment like how&nbsp;
much pain they’re in because of their arthritis.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:59.520
As much as Simons dreamt of this kind&nbsp;
of trading system - computers in the

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.720
eighties weren’t sophisticated enough&nbsp;
to handle a fully automated system.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.280
So, at the start, Renaissance relied on math

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:10.720
models AND human intuition to&nbsp;
trade…which proved problematic.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.640
His code-breaking colleague Leonard Baum&nbsp;
who now worked at Renaissance liked to buy

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.960
low but often clung to positions too long,&nbsp;
like in 1984, when he heavily invested in

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:26.560
U.S. bonds. But as the Reagan administration&nbsp;
began issuing so many bonds, prices plummeted.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:32.160
Simons recognized the limits of human judgment and&nbsp;
brought in James Ax, a friend from his PhD days at

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:37.680
UC Berkeley whom he recruited to Stony Brook to&nbsp;
build more sophisticated math models for trading.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.440
The team collected data from&nbsp;
as far back as the 1800s,

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:45.200
feeding them into computers,&nbsp;
to uncover overlooked patterns.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:50.400
Simons told a colleague: “If we have enough&nbsp;
data, I know we can make predictions.”

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.320
Simons and Ax launched a&nbsp;
new hedge fund, Medallion,

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:56.560
in honor of the prestigious&nbsp;
math awards they’d received.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.280
It would eventually become the most&nbsp;
successful hedge fund of all time.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:04.320
But at first, it struggled to make a profit.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:08.400
It didn’t help that Ax isolated himself&nbsp;
in his seaside estate in Malibu.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.600
His preference for the West Coast led&nbsp;
him to spearhead his new company Axcom,

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:15.960
a separate entity yet closely&nbsp;
connected to Renaissance.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.440
The turning point came when Elwyn Berlekamp who

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:22.360
had worked to crack codes with Simons&nbsp;
bought up Ax’s part of the business.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:25.920
He rebuilt the system to focus&nbsp;
on short-term trades which Ax

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:28.920
had resisted due to concerns over commission fees.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:34.600
Berlekamp figured “If you trade a lot, you&nbsp;
only need to be right 51 percent of the time.”

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.000
Many investors prefer long-term trades,

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.120
a sentiment Zuckerman describes&nbsp;
in his book this way :

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:44.600
“...like fishermen ignoring the guppies&nbsp;
in their nets, hoping for a bigger catch.”

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:49.680
Unlike these investors, Medallion honed in&nbsp;
on these 'guppies' — seeking to capitalize

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:53.000
on subtle inefficiencies that others overlooked.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:07:00.440
Don’t give up easily. Stick with&nbsp;
it. Stick with it. Not forever,

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:10.960
but really give it a chance to get where you’re&nbsp;
going. The final principle is: hope for good luck!

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:15.720
After a decade of adjusting their algorithms&nbsp;
aided by improved computer processing power,

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.400
Renaissance turned the corner in 1990.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:23.560
The Medallion fund posted gains after fees of 58%.

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:28.440
But Simons believed they could do&nbsp;
even better; he wanted 80% returns.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:31.920
Berlekamp thought he was crazy&nbsp;
and it all became too much for

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:34.840
him - he returned to teaching at UC Berkeley.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.400
Simons bought Berlekamp’s share of the company.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.440
Axcom, which had been a separate entity,

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:43.920
disbanded. Going forward, it was&nbsp;
just Renaissance Technologies.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.120
Simons beefed up the team some more.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:51.360
Henry Laufer, a mathematician from Stony Brook,&nbsp;
divided the trading day into five-minute intervals

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:57.400
instead of hourly, allowing Renaissance to quickly&nbsp;
detect and act on even shorter-term price changes.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.040
Their models were so successful at making&nbsp;
trades that they trusted their computers

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:06.320
to make decisions that didn’t even make&nbsp;
sense to them. There was however one issue.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:08.360
Renaissance was still very small.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:12.040
It only managed $45 million&nbsp;
after ten years in the business,

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:15.280
a quarter of the assets of rival firm D.E. Shaw.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.240
David Shaw was also using&nbsp;
computer models to trade.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.720
AND - he was trading stocks, unlike Renaissance.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:26.840
Simons knew that if he wanted to truly leave&nbsp;
a legacy, he needed to start trading stocks.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:29.560
He consolidated all operations in Long Island

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:34.160
and was about to embark on a&nbsp;
new path when tragedy struck.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:36.920
Simons’ son Paul, who had&nbsp;
battled a birth disorder,

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:40.920
was cycling in Stony Brook when&nbsp;
he was struck by a car and killed.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:44.040
The elderly driver was so&nbsp;
traumatized by what happened

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:47.200
that she died of a heart attack a few days later.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:52.120
The grief of losing a child caused&nbsp;
Simons to throw himself into his work.

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:56.280
In order to master the art of&nbsp;
trading stocks, he needed more help.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:59.280
Simons recruited Robert Mercer and Peter Brown,

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:03.920
renowned for their groundbreaking work&nbsp;
in transcribing speech into text at IBM.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:08.600
They saw language as a probabilistic game&nbsp;
where some words likely follow others.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:13.080
For instance, after "chocolate,"&nbsp;
"milk" is more probable than "cheese."

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:18.320
Similarly, Simons wanted to design a system&nbsp;
that could predict financial market trends.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:23.440
With their coding chops, Brown and Mercer crafted&nbsp;
a single automated stock trading system introduced

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:29.120
in 1995 complete with half a million lines of code&nbsp;
compared to tens of thousands in the old system.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:34.640
After Mercer and Brown mastered&nbsp;
stocks, the Medallion fund took off.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:43.160
In 2000, it achieved an astonishing 98.5% return&nbsp;
after fees, even amidst the dot-com bubble,

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:48.320
showcasing Renaissance's prowess&nbsp;
in capitalizing on inefficiencies.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:52.440
Simons rewarded his staff with&nbsp;
exotic vacations and bonuses.

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:56.480
These computer science nerds who were&nbsp;
in it for the challenge, not the money,

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:59.560
couldn’t help but reap the financial rewards.

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:02.205
Staff could personally invest in Medallion.

00:10:02.205 --> 00:10:02.280
Medallion is only available to employees.
The fund is capped at $10 billion.

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:05.280
In fact, as Medallion grew, it closed its doors

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:09.960
to outside investors in 2003 and&nbsp;
is only available to employees.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.960
The fund is capped at $10 billion.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:18.880
They bought so many mansions in one neighborhood&nbsp;
that it earned the nickname “Renaissance Riviera”

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:21.880
How exactly Renaissance has&nbsp;
managed to achieve stunning

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:26.200
results is a secret. Employees are&nbsp;
bound by non-disclosure agreements,

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:30.880
ensuring they remain silent about the&nbsp;
coding and algorithms Renaissance uses.

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:34.840
Many firms on Wall Street are trying&nbsp;
to crack the Renaissance code.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:39.720
JPMorgan Chase makes it mandatory for new&nbsp;
investment bankers and managers to learn coding.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:44.680
With advancements in technology and AI, more&nbsp;
data sets can be collected and analyzed.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:48.600
Even scrutinizing the tone of voice&nbsp;
executives use on conference calls.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:52.480
Monitoring which aisle shoppers spend&nbsp;
most of their time in grocery stores.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:59.040
Or analyzing social media posts to gauge sentiment&nbsp;
- by the way, let’s connect on X, I’m @Newsthink.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.640
Medallion is the most successful&nbsp;
hedge fund of all time.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.720
The returns are so high that it&nbsp;
charges a performance fee of 44%

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:12.000
Simons had achieved everything he wanted&nbsp;
to achieve in the world of investing.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.760
He thought about retiring.

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:17.840
But then…tragedy struck again.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:20.080
His son Nicholas wanted to become a doctor and

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:23.680
open a medical clinic in Nepal&nbsp;
to help its poorest people.

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:29.240
A week before he was scheduled to come&nbsp;
home, he drowned while freediving in Bali.

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:32.040
Simons had to bury a second child.

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:35.720
For a man who believed so&nbsp;
strongly in science and logic,

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:41.160
this unbearable loss was a reminder of&nbsp;
the inherent unpredictability of life.

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:45.760
He threw himself into his work once&nbsp;
again to distract him from his grief.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:49.040
Simons launched a larger fund,&nbsp;
the Renaissance Institutional

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:52.400
Equities Fund (RIEF), which is&nbsp;
available to outside investors.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:56.560
RIEF weathered the storm of the&nbsp;
2007 subprime mortgage crisis

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.680
triggered by high-risk loans given to&nbsp;
Americans with questionable credit.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:05.840
In an unusual move owing to extraordinary&nbsp;
circumstances, Simons felt the need for human

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:10.840
intervention, guiding his team to reduce&nbsp;
equity positions and build cash reserves.

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:18.200
Ultimately, Renaissance rebounded and during the&nbsp;
turmoil of 2008, Medallion boasted 82% in profits,

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:22.840
highlighting the sophistication and&nbsp;
adaptability of their trading systems.

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:27.240
Having achieved all that he wanted in the&nbsp;
world of investing, Simons stepped down

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:33.480
as CEO of Renaissance Technologies in 2009,&nbsp;
handing over the reins to Brown and Mercer.

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:38.520
Throughout his entire career, Simons maintained a&nbsp;
low public profile compared to some of his peers.

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:40.680
Yet the spotlight couldn’t help but shine on

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:44.960
Renaissance because of its&nbsp;
new co-CEO, Robert Mercer.

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:48.280
Mercer distrusted the government,&nbsp;
disapproved of the Clintons,

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:52.000
and questioned whether humans are&nbsp;
the primary cause of climate change.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.040
He emerged as one of Trump’s biggest financial

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:58.400
backers in 2016 as he felt only an&nbsp;
outsider could win the election.

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:04.000
The Mercer family also acquired a significant&nbsp;
stake in the conservative outlet Breitbart News.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:07.520
Mercer’s daughter Rebekah recommended&nbsp;
the head of Breitbart, Steve Bannon,

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:12.560
run Trump’s campaign, while Kellyan&nbsp;
Conway became a familiar face on TV.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:16.800
Together, Bannon and Conway helped&nbsp;
propel Trump to the White House.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:21.520
On the other hand, Simons was a significant&nbsp;
supporter of Hillary Clinton's campaign.

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:23.640
Despite their different political leanings,

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:27.680
Simons felt Mercer shouldn’t be&nbsp;
fired for his political actions.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:30.520
But then, it started affecting business.

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:35.000
Renaissance programmer David Magerman was&nbsp;
horrified by the election of Trump and

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:39.440
expressed concerns about Mercer’s involvement&nbsp;
to Zuckerman for the Wall Street Journal.

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:41.920
Investors shared similar concerns.

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:44.320
When the Baltimore City Fire and Police Employees’

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:49.960
Retirement System pulled $32 million from&nbsp;
Renaissance, the firm feared a mass exodus.

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:52.720
Simons suggested Mercer step down.

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:57.560
According to Zuckerman,&nbsp;
Mercer “looked sad and hurt.”

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:03.240
In November 2017, Mercer resigned as&nbsp;
co-CEO at Renaissance Technologies and

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:06.440
sold his stake in Breitbart News to his daughters.

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:07.560
As for Simons?

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:10.640
With a personal net worth of $27 billion,

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:15.520
he can afford to indulge in luxuries,&nbsp;
like a $100-million super-yacht.

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:19.000
Yet his wealth doesn’t just fund lavish pursuits.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:22.440
He’s donated $500 million&nbsp;
to Stony Brook University

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:27.000
Established a nonprofit that provides&nbsp;
exceptional math teachers with additional support

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:32.480
And started a charitable foundation that, among&nbsp;
other pursuits, is dedicated to autism research,

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:37.480
a cause close to his heart as his daughter&nbsp;
Audrey, with his second wife, is autistic.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:42.560
The Simons Foundation is also heavily focused&nbsp;
on discovering the origins of the universe.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:47.480
Its Observatory under construction in Chile’s&nbsp;
Atacama Desert will search for gravitational

